// L4 `G` — a bare `Chunk { … }`, with `hex_voxel` imported FIRST.
//
// ⚠ THIS FILE HAS MEANT THREE DIFFERENT THINGS, and it is the same six lines each time.
// Read the three together, because the sequence is the finding:
//
//   1. 2026-08-06 — both packages declared `Chunk`, and the bare name bound to whichever
//      was `use`d FIRST. So this file took `hex_voxel`'s and failed on a field that only
//      THEIRS has: `error: Unknown field Chunk.ck_cells` — the `Surface` diagnostic of
//      `L1`, verbatim, naming a field and never a collision. `H`, the same file with the
//      imports swapped, COMPILED. One source line, two meanings. loft#788.
//   2. 2026-08-07 — loft 2026.8.0 refused the bare name at BOTH orders and named the two
//      packages. The import order stopped deciding, which was the fix.
//   3. `L6.2` — ours is `VoxelChunk`, so `Chunk` is declared by exactly ONE package again.
//      The literal simply compiles and takes theirs, at either order, meaning the same
//      thing both times.
//
// The fields below are THEIRS (`ck_cells` exists on no other `Chunk` in the graph), so if
// this ever prints something other than theirs, a second `Chunk` is back in the graph.
use hex_voxel;
use hex_world;

fn main() {
  c = Chunk { ck_cx: 1, ck_cz: 2, ck_cells: [] };
  println("PROBE bare literal took THEIRS: {c.ck_cx}");
}
